- [Logging](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-logging.md): MSK Connect can write log events that you can use to debug your connector.
- [Monitoring MSK Connect](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/mkc-monitoring-overview.md): Monitor MSK Connect to maintain reliability, availability, and performance.

### [Examples](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-examples.md)

Examples for Amazon MSK Connect that demonstrate how to set up common connectors and configuration providers.

- [Set up Amazon S3 sink connector](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/mkc-S3sink-connector-example.md): This example shows how to set up the Confluent Amazon S3 sink connector for Amazon MSK connect.
- [Set up EventBridge Kafka sink connector](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/mkc-eventbridge-kafka-connector.md): Learn how to set up MSK Connect with EventBridge to stream data from Apache Kafka topics to EventBridge for building event-driven architectures.

### [Use Debezium source connector](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/mkc-debeziumsource-connector-example.md)

This example shows how to use the Debezium MySQL connector with a MySQL-compatible Amazon Aurora database as the source.

- [Complete prerequisites to use Debezium source connector](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/mkc-debeziumsource-connector-example-prereqs.md): Your connector must be able to access the internet so that it can interact with services such as AWS Secrets Manager that are outside of your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud.
- [Create a Debezium source connector](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-debeziumsource-connector-example-steps.md): This procedure describes how to create a Debezium source connector.
- [Update a Debezium connector configuration](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/mkc-debeziumsource-connector-update.md): To update the configuration of the Debezium connector, follow these steps:

### [Migrate to Amazon MSK Connect](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-migrating.md)

This section describes how to migrate your Apache Kafka connector application to Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka Connect (Amazon MSK Connect).

- [Understand internal topics used by Kafka Connect](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-kafka-connect-topics.md): An Apache Kafka Connect application that’s running in distributed mode stores its state by using internal topics in the Kafka cluster and group membership.
- [State management](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-state-management.md): By default, Amazon MSK Connect creates three separate topics in the Kafka cluster for each Amazon MSK Connector to store the connector’s configuration, offset, and status.
- [Migrate source connectors](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-migrate-source-connectors.md): Source connectors are Apache Kafka Connect applications that import records from external systems into Kafka.
- [Migrate sink connectors](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-migrate-sink-connectors.md): Sink connectors are Apache Kafka Connect applications that export data from Kafka to external systems.
- [Troubleshooting](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect-troubleshooting.md): The following information can help you troubleshoot problems that you might have while using MSK Connect.


## [MSK Replicator](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator.md)

### [Get started](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-get-started.md)

Review supported configurations and prerequisites, and create your first replicator.

- [Supported configurations](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-supported-configs.md): The following are requirements for cluster types, Kafka versions, instance types, and network configurations when using MSK Replicator.
- [Prerequisites](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-prerequisites.md): Before you create an MSK Replicator, ensure that you have the following prerequisites in place.
- [Prepare source and target clusters](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-prepare-clusters.md): Before creating an MSK Replicator, you need to prepare both a source cluster and a target cluster.
- [Create your first replicator](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-first-replicator.md): This walkthrough guides you through creating your first MSK Replicator between two MSK clusters using the AWS console.

### [Replicator operations](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-manage.md)

Create, edit, delete, and monitor MSK Replicators using the AWS console, CLI, or infrastructure as code.

### [Create a replicator](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-create-op.md)

Before you create an MSK Replicator, make sure you have the and have completed .

- [Using the console](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-create-console.md)
- [Using the CLI](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-create-cli.md): Use the create-replicator command to create an MSK Replicator.
- [Using CloudFormation/Terraform](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-create-cfn.md)
- [Edit a replicator](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-edit.md): You cannot change the source cluster, target cluster, Replicator starting position, or topic name replication configuration once the MSK Replicator has been created.
- [Delete a replicator](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-delete.md): You may need to delete an MSK Replicator if it fails to create (FAILED status).
- [Monitor replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-monitor.md): You can use Amazon CloudWatch in the target cluster Region to view metrics for ReplicationLatency, MessageLag, and ReplicatorThroughput at a topic and aggregate level for each MSK Replicator.

### [Replication patterns](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-patterns.md)

Set up same-region, cross-region, active-passive, and active-active replication patterns, and perform failover and failback operations.

- [Same-region replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-same-region.md): In same-region replication (SRR), both the source and target MSK clusters are in the same AWS Region.
- [Cross-region replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-cross-region.md): In cross-region replication (CRR), the source and target MSK clusters are in different AWS Regions.
- [Active-passive replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-active-passive.md): In an active-passive setup, only one MSK cluster at a time is actively serving streaming data, while the other cluster is on standby.
- [Active-active replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-active-active.md): In an active-active setup, both MSK clusters are actively serving reads and writes.
- [Failover to the secondary Region](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-when-failover.md): We recommend that you monitor replication latency in the secondary AWS Region using Amazon CloudWatch.
- [Planned failover](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-planned-failover.md): You can conduct a planned failover to test the resiliency of your application against an unexpected event in your primary AWS Region.
- [Unplanned failover](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-unplanned-failover.md): You can conduct an unplanned failover when there is a service event in the primary AWS Region and you want to temporarily redirect your traffic to the secondary Region.
- [Failback](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-failback.md): You can failback to the primary AWS Region after the service event in that Region has ended.

### [Migration](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-migration.md)

Migrate between Amazon MSK clusters, from self-managed Kafka to Amazon MSK, and from MirrorMaker 2 to Amazon MSK Replicator.

- [Migrate between Amazon MSK clusters](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-migrate-cluster.md): Amazon MSK Replicator supports migrations between Amazon MSK clusters in the same AWS account.

### [Migrate from self-managed Kafka](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-migrate-external.md)

You can use MSK Replicator to migrate Apache Kafka workloads from self-managed environments to Amazon MSK Provisioned clusters.

- [Self-managed Kafka prerequisites](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-external-prereqs.md)
- [API examples for self-managed Kafka](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-external-api-examples.md)
- [Migrate from MirrorMaker 2](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-migrate-mm2.md): To migrate from MirrorMaker (MM2) to MSK Replicator, follow these steps:
- [Assess readiness with AI](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-migrate-ai-assist.md): Before you begin a migration, you can use AI-assisted tooling to evaluate your source Apache Kafka cluster and plan your target Amazon MSK deployment.
- [Cross-account migration](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-cross-account.md): For migrations across different AWS accounts, you must use Apache MirrorMaker 2.0.

### [Key concepts](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-concepts.md)

Understand how Amazon MSK Replicator works, including data replication, topic naming, metadata replication, consumer group offset syncing, and network requirements.

- [How replication works](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-how-replication-works.md): When you create a Replicator, MSK Replicator deploys all required resources in the target cluster's AWS Region to optimize for data replication latency.
- [Topic naming](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-topic-naming.md): MSK Replicator has two topic name configuration modes: Prefixed (default) or Identical topic name replication.
- [Metadata and ACL replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-metadata-acl.md): MSK Replicator supports copying metadata from the source cluster to the target cluster.
- [Consumer group offset synchronization](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-bidirectional-offset-sync.md): MSK Replicator can synchronize consumer group offsets from a source cluster to a target cluster, allowing consumers to switch clusters and resume processing without skipping records.
- [Network requirements](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-network-reqs.md): MSK Replicator uses IAM access control to connect to your clusters, which uses port 9098.

### [Security](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-security.md)

Understand the security model for Amazon MSK Replicator, including the service execution role, encryption, and IAM permissions.

### [Service execution role (SER)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-ser.md)

MSK Replicator uses a service execution role (SER) to read from your source cluster and write to your target cluster.

- [Additional SER permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-ser-additional-perms.md): The AWSMSKReplicatorExecutionRole managed policy covers cluster, topic, and consumer group permissions for IAM auth.

### [Service execution role permissions reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-permissions-reference.md)

Understand the minimal IAM permissions and Kafka access control lists (ACLs) required by the Amazon MSK Replicator service execution role for each replicator feature.

- [Topic replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-perms-topic-replication.md): Use Amazon MSK Replicator to replicate topics between clusters.
- [Topic configuration replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-perms-topic-config-replication.md): When copyTopicConfigurations is enabled (the default), the replicator reads topic-level configurations from the source cluster.
- [ACL replication](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-perms-acl-replication.md): When copyAccessControlListsForTopics is enabled (the default), the replicator copies access control lists from the source cluster to the target cluster.
- [Consumer group offset sync](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-perms-consumer-group-sync.md): When synchroniseConsumerGroupOffsets is enabled (the default), the replicator translates consumer group offsets from the source cluster and writes them to the target cluster.
- [Log delivery](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-perms-log-delivery.md): Grant log delivery permissions to the caller role (the IAM principal that calls CreateReplicator), not to the service execution role.
- [Combined policy example](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-perms-combined-example.md): The following example shows a complete service execution role policy for a replicator with all features enabled, including topic configuration replication and LEGACY offset sync mode.
- [Encryption](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-encryption.md): All communication and data between MSK Replicator and your clusters is always encrypted in-transit.

### [Required IAM permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-create-iam-perms.md)

The IAM principal (user or role) that calls CreateReplicator needs the permissions described in this section.

- [Log delivery permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-create-iam-perms-logs.md): If you configure log delivery on the Replicator, append the appropriate statements below to the base policy.
- [IAM permissions reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-iam-ref.md): The following table summarizes the IAM actions that the API caller (the IAM principal that calls CreateReplicator and other replicator APIs) needs.
- [Pricing](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-pricing.md): Understand the pricing model for Amazon MSK Replicator.

### [Observability](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-observability.md)

Monitor Amazon MSK Replicator using Amazon CloudWatch metrics and troubleshoot common issues.

- [Metrics reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-metrics-ref.md): The following metrics describes performance or connection metrics for the MSK Replicator.
- [Monitor replication from self-managed Kafka clusters](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-monitor-external.md): After you create an MSK Replicator to replicate data from a self-managed Apache Kafka cluster to an Amazon MSK Provisioned cluster, monitor the following Amazon CloudWatch metrics to track replication progress and determine when your applications are ready to migrate.
- [MSK Replicator logs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-logs.md): MSK Replicator supports forwarding operational logs to help you monitor and troubleshoot replication.

### [Best practices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-best-practices.md)

Best practices for sizing, throughput management, and retention when using Amazon MSK Replicator.

- [Sizing and partitions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-bp-sizing.md): The number of partitions on your source and target MSK clusters directly impacts replication performance.
- [Managing throughput with Kafka quotas](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-bp-quotas.md): Since MSK Replicator acts as a consumer for your source cluster, replication can cause other consumers to be throttled on your source cluster.
- [Retention period](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-bp-retention.md): You can set the log retention period for MSK Provisioned and Serverless clusters.
- [Cluster configuration changes after Replicator creation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-post-creation-config.md)
- [Considerations for multi-Region applications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-bp-multi-region.md): When building multi-Region Apache Kafka applications with MSK Replicator, keep the following in mind:
- [Troubleshooting](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-troubleshooting.md): The following information can help you troubleshoot problems with MSK Replicator.


## [MSK streaming tables to Apache Iceberg (New)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg.md)

- [Key concepts](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-concepts.md): This section describes the key concepts behind Amazon MSK Data Delivery.

### [Get started](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-getting-started.md)

- [Supported configurations](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-supported-configs.md)
- [Prerequisites](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-prerequisites.md)
- [Create your first Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-first-channel.md)

### [IAM permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-iam.md)

There are two distinct sets of permissions: the Channel lifecycle management permissions that you need to create and manage Channels, and the service execution role that the Channel assumes to deliver data.

- [Channel lifecycle management permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-iam-lifecycle.md): The following permissions are for the IAM principal (the user or role) you use to create, update, describe, delete, and list Channels.
- [Service execution role](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-iam-service-role.md): A Channel assumes a service execution role to deliver data.

### [Manage Channels](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-manage.md)

Resource states: CREATING, ACTIVE, UPDATING, DELETING, FAILED, SUSPENDING, SUSPENDED.

- [Create a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-create.md)
- [Describe a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-describe.md)
- [Update a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-update.md): You can modify only the data freshness interval (DataFreshnessInSeconds, 300–900).
- [Delete a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-delete.md): Deleting a Channel stops delivery from the Kafka topic to the destination.
- [List Channels](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-list.md)

### [Iceberg table behaviors](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-behaviors.md)

This section describes how a Channel maps schemas and manages the Apache Iceberg table it creates in Amazon S3 Tables.

- [Schema type mapping](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-schema-mapping.md): When you register a schema in the AWS Glue Schema Registry, the Channel maps the JSON Schema types to Iceberg column types as follows.
- [Table type](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-table-type.md): The Iceberg table that a Channel creates in S3 Tables is managed by the Amazon MSK service.
- [Table maintenance](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-maintenance.md): S3 Tables provides optional automated table maintenance jobs — compaction, snapshot expiration, and unreferenced file cleanup.
- [Table attributes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-attributes.md): The Iceberg table a Channel creates has the following attributes (designed for governance and compliance):
- [Core library version](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-version.md)

### [Security](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-security.md)

- [Encryption in transit](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-security-transit.md): A Channel encrypts all data in transit between Amazon MSK Express brokers and the Channel service using TLS 1.2 or higher.
- [Encryption at rest](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-security-rest.md): Data delivered to S3 Table buckets is encrypted at rest using the destination bucket's default encryption settings:
- [IAM and access control](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-security-iam.md): A Channel uses IAM roles for authorization.

### [Monitoring](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-monitoring.md)

A Channel publishes data-delivery metrics to Amazon CloudWatch under the AWS/Kafka namespace.

- [Amazon CloudWatch metrics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-metrics.md)
- [Metric dimensions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-metric-dimensions.md)
- [Recommended alarms](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-alarms.md)
- [Metrics in the console](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-viewing-metrics.md)

### [Logging](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-logging.md)

- [CloudTrail logging](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-cloudtrail.md): All Channel API calls are logged in AWS CloudTrail: CreateChannel, DescribeChannel, UpdateChannel, DeleteChannel, ListChannels.
- [Amazon CloudWatch Logs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-cw-logs.md): A Channel can publish operational logs to a Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group, including schema resolution events, delivery attempts and outcomes, and error details for failed deliveries.

### [Best practices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-bestpractices.md)

- [Throughput and data freshness](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-bp-throughput.md)
- [Schema management](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-bp-schema.md)
- [Security](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-bp-security.md)

### [Troubleshooting](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-troubleshooting.md)

- [Channel stuck in CREATING state](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-creating.md)
- [Checking operation status](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-operation-status.md)
- [Data freshness higher than configured](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-freshness.md)
- [Throughput exceeds the supported limit](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-throughput.md)
- [Failed records (FailedRowCount greater than 0)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-failed-records.md)
- [No data appearing in the table](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-no-data.md)
- [Channel is suspended](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-suspended.md)
- [Delivery stops after a schema change](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-schema-change.md)
- [Permission denied errors in Amazon CloudWatch Logs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-permission-denied.md)
- [Channel not available on cluster](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-iceberg-ts-not-available.md)


## [MSK data delivery to Amazon S3 (New)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3.md)

- [Key concepts](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-concepts.md): This section describes the key concepts behind Amazon MSK Data Delivery.

### [Get started](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-getting-started.md)

- [Supported configurations](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-supported-configs.md)
- [Prerequisites](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-prerequisites.md)
- [Create your first Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-first-channel.md)

### [IAM permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-iam.md)

There are two distinct sets of permissions: the Channel lifecycle management permissions that you need to create and manage Channels, and the service execution role that the Channel assumes to deliver data.

- [Channel lifecycle management permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-iam-lifecycle.md): The following permissions are for the IAM principal (the user or role) you use to create, update, describe, delete, and list Channels.
- [Service execution role](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-iam-service-role.md): A Channel assumes a service execution role to deliver data.

### [Manage Channels](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-manage.md)

Resource states: CREATING, ACTIVE, UPDATING, DELETING, FAILED, SUSPENDING, SUSPENDED.

- [Create a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-create.md)
- [Describe a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-describe.md)
- [Update a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-update.md): You can modify only the data freshness interval (DataFreshnessInSeconds, 300–900).
- [Delete a Channel](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-delete.md): Deleting a Channel stops delivery from the Kafka topic to the destination.
- [List Channels](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-list.md)

### [Output key template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-output-key-template.md)

For Amazon S3 general purpose buckets, the output key template controls the S3 object key (path and file name) of each delivered object.

- [Variables](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-template-variables.md): Variables use the !{name} syntax.
- [Partitioning](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-template-partitioning.md)
- [Rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-template-rules.md): If you provide a template, it must satisfy all of the following.
- [Examples](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-template-examples.md)

### [Security](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-security.md)

- [Encryption in transit](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-security-transit.md): A Channel encrypts all data in transit between Amazon MSK Express brokers and the Channel service using TLS 1.2 or higher.
- [Encryption at rest](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-security-rest.md): Data delivered to general-purpose S3 buckets is encrypted at rest using the destination bucket's default encryption settings:
- [IAM and access control](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-security-iam.md): A Channel uses IAM roles for authorization.

### [Monitoring](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-monitoring.md)

A Channel publishes data-delivery metrics to Amazon CloudWatch under the AWS/Kafka namespace.

- [Amazon CloudWatch metrics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-metrics.md)
- [Metric dimensions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-metric-dimensions.md)
- [Recommended alarms](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-alarms.md)
- [Metrics in the console](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-viewing-metrics.md)

### [Logging](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-logging.md)

- [CloudTrail logging](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-cloudtrail.md): All Channel API calls are logged in AWS CloudTrail: CreateChannel, DescribeChannel, UpdateChannel, DeleteChannel, ListChannels.
- [Amazon CloudWatch Logs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-cw-logs.md): A Channel can publish operational logs to a Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group, including schema resolution events, delivery attempts and outcomes, and error details for failed deliveries.

### [Best practices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-bestpractices.md)

- [Throughput and data freshness](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-bp-throughput.md)
- [Object layout](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-bp-layout.md)
- [Security](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-data-delivery-s3-bp-security.md)

